---
// The hub: four live pages, one door to the rest. Layout is Pico CSS
// (classless, vendored at /vendor/pico.min.css) so there is no CSS of ours to
// think about beyond the frame heights.
import { buildHub } from "../lib/pages.js";
import { lanUrls, tunnelInfo } from "../lib/tunnel.js";

import AppNav from "../components/AppNav.astro";
const hub = buildHub();
const tunnel = tunnelInfo();
// The LAN list is for the phone at home and the person at the Mac. On a public
// hostname it would print this machine's private addresses to a stranger, so
// it is shown only when the request came from localhost.
const host = Astro.request.headers.get("host") ?? "";
const isLocalHost = /^(localhost|127\.0\.0\.1|\[::1\])(:\d+)?$/.test(host);
const lan = isLocalHost ? lanUrls() : [];
const app = hub.app;
const older = hub.total - app.length;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="light"><head><meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Curve Hub: draft A</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/vendor/pico.min.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/vendor/curve-lab-theme.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/vendor/app-shell.css">
<style is:global>
/* only what Pico cannot know: the window height, the still, and the two-column menu */
.app-grid{display:grid;gap:1.25rem;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(26rem,1fr))}
.app-grid article{margin:0}
.app-grid h3{margin:0}
.app-grid .purpose{font-size:.8rem;color:var(--pico-muted-color);margin:.2rem 0 .6rem}
.app-grid .open{font-size:.78rem}
.window{position:relative;height:30rem;background:#fff}
.window .shot{display:block;width:100%;height:100%}
.window img{display:block;width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:cover;object-position:top left}
.window iframe{display:block;width:100%;height:100%;border:0;background:#fff}
.phone{font-size:.85rem}
.hint{font-size:.72rem;color:var(--pico-muted-color);margin:.35rem .1rem 0}
/* the desks: the item, then its note in italics on the same line — wider, never longer */
.desks{display:grid;grid-template-columns:max-content max-content;gap:.3rem 1.2rem;
  margin:.7rem 0 0;padding:0;list-style:none;max-width:100%;overflow-x:auto}
.desks li{display:contents}
.desks a{white-space:nowrap}
.desks em{font-style:italic;color:var(--pico-muted-color);white-space:nowrap}
@media (max-width:600px){.window{height:22rem}}
</style>
</head><body>
<div class="draft-a" style="position:fixed;right:.75rem;bottom:.75rem;z-index:9999;background:#1b1b1b;color:#fff;font:12px/1.4 system-ui,sans-serif;padding:.35rem .6rem;border-radius:.25rem;opacity:.9">draft A</div>
<AppNav title="the hub" />
<main id="main" class="container">
  <h1>curve-lab</h1>
  <p>This Astro app (port 4321) is the product; the small Python server on
    port 8003 is the render MVP, not the deliverable. Four pages, a still each, live
    when the window is reached. {older} older pages behind one link.</p>
  <p class="hint">Each window opens as a still; the live page loads when the window is reached,
    and the small print under it says which. Click a still to load it now.</p>
  <p class="phone">{tunnel.url
    ? <>phone: <a href={tunnel.url}>{tunnel.url}</a></>
    : <>phone: no tunnel running — <code>scripts/service.sh status</code></>}
    {lan.length ? <> · home: <a href={lan[0]}>{lan[0]}</a></> : null}</p>

  <div class="app-grid">
    {app.map((page) => (
      <article>
        <header>
          <h3>{page.title}</h3>
          <p class="purpose">{page.purpose}</p>
          <a class="open" href={page.src} target="_blank" rel="noopener">open in a tab ↗</a>
        </header>
        <div class="window" data-window data-src={page.src} data-title={page.title}>
          <a class="shot" href={page.src} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            <img src={`/hub/${page.key}.png`} width="1200" height="800"
              alt={`a still of ${page.title}`} loading="lazy" />
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="hint" data-hint>still picture · the live page loads when the window is reached</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>

    <article style="margin-top:2rem">
    <h2>The desks</h2>
    <p>Every catalog page, as a plain link — they are relative, so they work from whatever
      address you opened this on.</p>
    <ul class="desks">
      <li><a href="/plates">the print plates</a><em>300 dpi, two greys, a dot at every rail crossing</em></li>
      <li><a href="/figures">every curve</a><em>one figure per record, the whole collection</em></li>
      <li><a href="/names">the naming desk</a><em>names, stars and the chosen curves</em></li>
      <li><a href="/hybrids">the hybrid desk</a><em>the shortlist, with turns and mirrors</em></li>
      <li><a href="/hybrids-wonk">the wonks</a><em>the staging page, a wonk and its pieces</em></li>
      <li><a href="/prime-mover">the prime mover</a><em>the walk, and the circle taken out</em></li>
      <li><a href="/sandbox">the sandboxes</a><em>the visual adder and the quadrature</em></li>
      <li><a href="/everything">everything</a><em>every page, one list, by kind</em></li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-top:2rem">
    <h2>The logic of beauty</h2>
    <p>Beauty is not an optional accessory.</p>
    <p>Take a simple black square on a white background, say a piece of paper. Stare
      at the square for a little, then look at the white part of the paper. You see a
      floating square. If you just look at the square continuously, you notice the
      edges seem to have the same effect as when you looked at the white part of the
      paper: a halo effect. This is because of the way our eye is constructed; the
      receptors get tired, and so a shadow shape is formed.</p>
    <p>When we add a beautiful frame to the square, we are softening the effect of
      tiredness in our eyes. The halo effect is swallowed up by the ornate part of the
      frame. Frames are often gold coloured not to show richness, but because the halo
      often has a golden or reddish-brown luminance.</p>
    <p>The same with a diploma on the wall, with an engraved outside edge — it makes it
      easier to look at.</p>
    <p>The line weight has to vary, and different colours help as well. This keeps the
      halo effect to a minimum.</p>
    <p>When we look at a window, we get the same effect in reverse: the light from the
      window creates a halo or shadow effect when we look away. Stained glass windows
      soften that effect, when they are done right.</p>
    <p>Another thing beauty does is orient us. Usually they help us retain balance by
      literally showing us which way is up. Often beautiful designs are symmetric
      side to side but they are different on top than they are on the bottom.</p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-top:2rem">
    <h2>There's more…</h2>
    <p>Everything else is kept and still working: the generators, the degree ladder's
      notes, the terminal and junction proofs, the catalogues. {older} pages in all,
      grouped by what you are looking for, so this page stays short and the four
      above stay at the top.</p>
    <p><a class="button" href="/everything" role="button">the full page list →</a></p>
  </article>
</main>
<script>import "../scripts/hub-windows.js";</script>
</body></html>
